Here are the three methods from the aarch64 MacroAssembler:

## `add128`

Takes low/high destination and low/high left/right operands. It emits two instructions:

```rust
fn add128(
    &mut self,
    dst_lo: WritableReg,
    dst_hi: WritableReg,
    lhs_lo: Reg,
    lhs_hi: Reg,
    rhs_lo: Reg,
    rhs_hi: Reg,
) -> Result<()> {
    self.asm.adds_rrr(rhs_lo, lhs_lo, dst_lo, OperandSize::S64);
    self.asm.adc_rrr(rhs_hi, lhs_hi, dst_hi, OperandSize::S64);
    Ok(())
}
```

The low half uses `adds_rrr` (setting flags), and the high half uses `adc_rrr` (add-with-carry), both at `S64`.

## `sub128`

Symmetric to `add128`, using subtract instructions:

```rust
fn sub128(
    &mut self,
    dst_lo: WritableReg,
    dst_hi: WritableReg,
    lhs_lo: Reg,
    lhs_hi: Reg,
    rhs_lo: Reg,
    rhs_hi: Reg,
) -> Result<()> {
    self.asm.subs_rrr(rhs_lo, lhs_lo, dst_lo, OperandSize::S64);
    self.asm.sbc_rrr(rhs_hi, lhs_hi, dst_hi, OperandSize::S64);
    Ok(())
}
```

The low half uses `subs_rrr` (setting flags), and the high half uses `sbc_rrr` (subtract-with-carry), both at `S64`.

## `mul_wide`

Register allocation order: `rhs` is popped first via `pop_to_reg`, then `lhs`, then a high-half destination register is obtained via `any_gpr`.

```rust
fn mul_wide(
    &mut self,
    context: &mut CodeGenContext<Emission>,
    kind: MulWideKind,
) -> Result<()> {
    let rhs = context.pop_to_reg(self, None)?;
    let lhs = context.pop_to_reg(self, None)?;
    let dst_hi = context.any_gpr(self)?;

    // Emit the high-half multiply first since the low-half multiply may
    // alias `lhs` or `rhs` as its destination.
    match kind {
        MulWideKind::Signed => self.asm.smulh_rrr(rhs.reg, lhs.reg, writable!(dst_hi)),
        MulWideKind::Unsigned => self.asm.umulh_rrr(rhs.reg, lhs.reg, writable!(dst_hi)),
    }
    self.asm
        .mul_rrr(rhs.reg, lhs.reg, writable!(lhs.reg), OperandSize::S64);

    context.free_reg(rhs);
    context.stack.push(lhs.into());
    context.stack.push(Val::Reg(TypedReg::i64(dst_hi)));
